<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raqqada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">876</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raqqada was a palace city near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, founded in 876 by the Aghlabid emir Ibrahim II as his residence. In 909 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama</a> army of the Fatimid movement took it, and in January 910 Abdallah al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> there. It remained a Fatimid seat until the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a>. Its site now houses a museum of Islamic art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raqqada.jpg" alt="Raqqada" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IssamBarhoumi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bassins_Arabe_de_Raqada_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
